  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">KemI Technical Assistance to ZEMA  on Pesticide Management</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">KemI Technical Assistance to ZEMA  on Pesticide Management</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">Projektets &#xf6;vergripande m&#xe5;l &#xe4;r att minska riskerna f&#xf6;r milj&#xf6;n och m&#xe4;nniskors och djurs h&#xe4;lsa genom f&#xf6;rb&#xe4;ttrad anv&#xe4;ndning av bek&#xe4;mpningsmedel i Zambia. Detta f&#xf6;rv&#xe4;ntas uppn&#xe5;s genom att den svenska Kemikalieinspektionen (KemI) st&#xf6;djer den zambiska Kemikalieinspektionen &quot;Zambia Environmental Management Agency&quot; (ZEMA) i genomf&#xf6;randet av en ny kemikalielagstiftning i Zambia, fr&#xe4;mst genom   kapacitetsutveckling av ZEMA, inklusive en l&#xe5;ngtidsexpert fr&#xe5;n KemI baserad i Zambia. St&#xf6;det inkluderar &#xe4;ven utveckling av den zambiska lagstiftningen, till exempel inom interna operativa f&#xf6;rfaranden, en samordningsmekanism mellan ansvariga ministerier / byr&#xe5;er / andra relevanta intressenter samt riktlinjer f&#xf6;r genomf&#xf6;randet av lagstiftningen. Utbildning av personal, b&#xe5;de registrerare och inspekt&#xf6;rer, kommer att genomf&#xf6;ras av KemI, som &#xe4;ven kommer att jobba f&#xf6;r att &#xf6;ka medvetenheten hos olika intressenter s&#xe5; som f&#xf6;retag inom kemikaliesektorn. 

Sidas avtalspartner &#xe4;r den svenska Kemikalieinspektionen (KemI), en central tillsynsmyndighet under Milj&#xf6;departementet med ansvar f&#xf6;r &#xe4;renden och samordning av bland annat kemiska produkter. 

Den totala budgeten f&#xf6;r insatsen &#xe4;r 13,3 miljoner SEK. Projektet kommer att p&#xe5;g&#xe5; i tre &#xe5;r, med start i juni 2020. Ett avtal kommer att undertecknas mellan Sida och KemI vid beslut. Avtalet &#xe4;r s&#xe4;rskilt utvecklat av Sida f&#xf6;r st&#xf6;d till svenska myndigheter och har &#xe4;ven godk&#xe4;nts av KemIs juridiska avdelning. Sidas kontaktperson f&#xf6;r KemI, Thomas Alveteg, har ocks&#xe5; varit involverad i processen.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The proposed intervention aims at reducing the risks to human and animal health and the environment from the use of pesticides by supporting the implementation of new and subsidiary chemicals legislation in Zambia. This will be achieved through the development of institutional capacity of the Zambia Environmental Management Agency (ZEMA) to ensure that the population and the environment is not harmed by building a robust authorization scheme. To support this, new and amended subsidiary legislation and certain administrative tools need to be developed such as internal standard operating procedures, a coordination mechanism between responsible ministries/agencies/other relevant stakeholders and guidelines for implementation of the legislation. Training of staff, both registrars and inspectors, as well as raising awareness among various stakeholders is crucial for strengthening the institutional capacity and contributes to compliance. A long-term adviser from KemI will be placed in Zambia, with the aim to strengthen the project.

The agreement partner is the Swedish Chemicals Agency (KemI), a supervisory authority under the Swedish Government, who work to ensure that the companies&apos; and society&apos;s chemicals control is managed in a good way.

The total budget for the proposed intervention is SEK 13,3 million. The project will run for three years, with start in June 2020. A Grant Agreement will be signed between Sida and KemI. The Grant Agreement is specifically developed by Sida for support to Swedish Authorities and has been approved by KemI&#x2019;s legal department. Sida&#x2019;s focal point for KemI, Thomas Alveteg, has also been involved in the process.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The overall objective of the proposed collaboration is to contribute to increased institutional capacity and better practices for the management of pesticides to safeguard human health and the environment. The following outcomes are envisaged from the collaboration:
1. A revised registration process for pesticides in Zambia to support registration of efficacious products that will not cause unacceptable harm to human health and the environment is established and the capacity of ZEMA staff and other relevant institutions has increased.
2. ZEMA&#x2019;s management of highly hazardous pesticides has improved.
3. An IT system for registration of pesticides is established and the information on ZEMA&#x2019;s website is further developed.</narrative>
  </description>
